# example-plugin

The smallest viable McHarbor plugin. No adapters, no routes, no widgets. Renders one form
field and emits a single audit record on Init + Shutdown.

This directory was scaffolded by `mcharbor plugin dev new example-plugin`, which copies the
template embedded in the CLI binary and rewrites the slug in every file that names it.

## Layout

```
example-plugin/
├── plugin.yaml                       # manifest (the host's contract)
├── README.md                         # this file
├── go.mod                            # Go module for the plugin binary
├── backend/
│   └── main.go                       # exports the `Plugin` symbol
├── frontend/
│   ├── package.json                  # Vite + React deps
│   ├── tsconfig.json
│   ├── vite-plugin.config.ts
│   └── src/
│       └── index.tsx                 # UMD bundle entry
├── icons/
│   └── plugin.svg
└── i18n/
    └── en/
        └── common.json
```

## What to edit first

1. `plugin.yaml` — `name`, `description`, and the `mcharbor` version range. The `slug` is
   already correct; changing it means changing it in all four places the loader compares
   (see `.claude/rules/versioning.md`).
2. `backend/main.go` — `manifest.Name`, and the `Register*` methods this plugin actually
   needs. Each one is a no-op stub.
3. `frontend/src/index.tsx` — the config form the operator sees.
4. `i18n/en/common.json`, then the other five locales. Every user-facing string goes
   through `t(...)`; a key must exist in all six before the call site does
   (`.claude/rules/i18n-keys.md`).

Do not copy a sibling plugin to start another one. A sibling carries its vendor quirks, its
bugs, and whatever pattern was current when it was written — run
`mcharbor plugin dev new <slug>` instead (`.claude/rules/plugin-authoring.md`).

## Build

```bash
mcharbor plugin dev validate example-plugin
mcharbor plugin dev package example-plugin
```

`validate` is worth running first: it catches a route the manifest never declares, which
gets no permission middleware at all. To build by hand instead:

```bash
cd src/plugins/Plugins/example-plugin/frontend
npm install && npm run build          # writes ../example-plugin.umd.cjs
cd ../backend
go build -buildmode=plugin -o ../example-plugin.so .
```

A built plugin reaches a running instance only after
`docker compose up -d --force-recreate mcharbor`.

## What the host does

- `plugin.Open` loads the `.so` and resolves the `Plugin` symbol.
- The runtime walks all seven `Register*` methods — each is a no-op here.
- `Init(ctx, host)` is called, and this plugin emits one audit record.
- `Shutdown(ctx)` is called on graceful stop, and emits another.
- The UMD bundle loads on demand when the operator opens the plugin's config form, through
  the `<PluginSlot slot="plugin.config_form.example-plugin">` outlet.
